{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## The fast gates do not guard this class — and the guard's population WAS widened\n\nRecording this because it generalises past this one fix.\n\n**The fast gates are blind here.** `make lint`, `make typecheck` and the complexity ratchet were\nALL green with the leaking assertion in the tree, both before and after. Nothing in the\ncommit-time gate chain can see this class: it is not a style violation, not a type error, and\nnot a complexity increase. Only the full suite catches it — and only because someone wrote an\noracle for it.\n\n**Two independent hits in one session.** A second agent hit the identical failure class today on\nan unrelated ticket (a plain `dict(os.environ)` in a held-out test), on a host that demonstrably\ncarries live Jira credentials and an `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`. Different route, same hazard, same day,\nno contact between the two. Two independent occurrences in one session is not coincidence — it\nsays the pattern is easy to write by reflex and that vigilance is not a control.\n\n**What prevents the third occurrence.** This change did NOT only fix the single instance. The new\noracle carries an AST audit that walks `tests/**` — the whole tree, every file, on every suite\nrun — so the guarded population is now the entire test suite rather than the one site named in\nthis ticket. Any future `assert` that names `os.environ` itself, anywhere in `tests/`, fails the\nbuild with a file:line. That widening is the durable part of the fix; the one-line edit at the\nop-cert site is the smaller half.\n\nCoverage of the hazard class is now three complementary oracles, each policing all of `tests/**`:\n\n| route | oracle |\n| --- | --- |\n| fixture-argument reprs | `test_fixture_env_repr_security.py` |\n| subprocess environment dicts | `test_subprocess_env_repr_security.py` |\n| assertion operands | `test_environ_assert_repr_security.py` (this change) |\n\nRemaining known gap, not closed here and not claimed to be: a raw environment reaching output by\nsome fourth route — e.g. an explicit `print`/`logging` call, or a `pytest.fail(str(os.environ))`.\nWorth a ticket if a fourth instance appears; three oracles for three observed routes is the\nevidence-proportionate stopping point today.\n\n**Caveat on the guards' own limits.** All three are pytest-resident, so they only fire when the\nsuite runs. They are portable (no CI dependency), but they are a suite-time control, not a\ncommit-time one. Anyone wanting commit-time enforcement would need an entry in the\n`scripts/check_*` chain; that was deliberately not added here, because the CI-only trigger of the\nexisting `check_wall_clock_asserts.py` step is exactly the portability shape\n`project.portability` warns against."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787269467568972001,"uuid":"1c011f2e-fadb-4084-a78e-b2b1b526ac2f"}